"## Model\n\n**Likelihood:** y | θ ~ Binomial(n, θ)\n\n**Prior:** θ ~ Beta(α, β)\n\n**Data:** y = 7 successes out of n = 10 trials\n\n**Prior hyperparameters:** α = 2, β = 2\n\n## Input Validation\n\n| Input | Value | Requirement | Check |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| y | 7 | integer, 0 ≤ y | ✓ non-negative integer |\n| n | 10 | integer, n ≥ 1 | ✓ positive integer |\n| y vs. n | 7 ≤ 10 | successes cannot exceed trials | ✓ consistent |\n| α | 2 | real, α > 0 | ✓ strictly positive |\n| β | 2 | real, β > 0 | ✓ strictly positive |\n\n**Verification details:**\n\n- **y = 7** — a non-negative integer, appropriate for a count of successes. ✓\n- **n = 10** — a positive integer, appropriate for a trial count. ✓\n- **y ≤ n** — 7 ≤ 10, so the observed count is achievable under Binomial(10, θ). ✓\n- **α = 2, β = 2** — both strictly positive, so Beta(2, 2) is a proper density on (0, 1) (integrates to 1). ✓\n\n**Conclusion:** All inputs are of the correct type and within valid ranges. No issues found — the model is well-posed and we proceed.\n\nSince this is a conjugate setup, the posterior will be Beta(α + y, β + n − y) = Beta(9, 5) whenever you're ready for the inference step."